---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
const blocked = [
  ["The two Brisbane immigration files","NAA: J25, 1971/9830 and J25, 1972/4964",
   "Paper only, held in Brisbane, not digitised. Case files of this era normally carry the sponsor, the addresses, the occupations, and often photographs. 1972/4964 is already marked Open.",
   "Anyone can order a copy through the National Archives of Australia."],
  ["The restricted Caserta images","FamilySearch, several Caserta collections",
   "Some record images return “This record can only be displayed on certain accounts.” The index and the machine transcriptions are open; the scans are not.",
   "A visit to a FamilySearch centre or an affiliate library unlocks them."],
  ["The two un-scanned volumes","Archivio di Stato di Benevento",
   "Arpaia births 1895 and Forchia births 1897 are not digitised — the shelf-marks run consecutively and skip those years. Between them they hold Angelo Zampiello's birth and Filomena Annecchino's, the two this archive cannot prove.",
   "Ordering or reading those two volumes at Benevento. See the Antenati page."],
  ["The processetti","Antenati — 258 volumes across four towns",
   "Marriage supplement files: birth extracts, parental consents, and death certificates of parents already dead. One file can carry two generations backwards. None have been read.",
   "Time, and a reader of Italian record hands. This is the largest unworked seam in the project."],
  ["The Museo Alfonsiano, Arienzo","The old bishop's palace beside Sant'Andrea",
   "It stands next to the church in which this family was baptised for two centuries, and may hold or know of parish material that was never filmed.",
   "A letter, or a visit."],
  ["DNA","MyHeritage / 23andMe",
   "Matches on the Falco and Zampiello sides could confirm or break the Annecchino descent below, and could find living cousins in Campania. No register can do that.",
   "The family already has tests in play; the matches need working through."],
];
const questions = [
  ["Who was Matteo Falco's father?","The line's own frontier",
   "Matteo was an adult by 1759 and fathering children from 1767. His marriage to Francesca Crisci — which would name his father — should sit in the Arienzo marriage register between about 1760 and 1766. It was searched for across 2,056 transcribed pages and not found. His baptism would fall in the gap the baptism register leaves between 1738 and 1761.",
   "Try the bishop's duplicate series, the Stato delle Anime, or Antenati."],
  ["Is Francesco Falco of Paolisi an ancestor?","1714–1726",
   "The earliest Arienzo Falco household with a stated origin. The dates would allow him to be Matteo's grandfather. No document joins them, and this archive has not joined them.",
   "The 1672–1738 baptism register, read properly."],
  ["Filomena Annecchino's act of birth","10 April 1897, Forchia",
   "The descent from Carmine Annecchino and Maria Carfora fits the tree exactly — including the grandfather's name — but her own birth record was not found. The harvest is capped by FamilySearch's paging limit and by fuzzy matching across Annecchino, Annecchini and Anicchino.",
   "Full-text search on the Benevento collection, or the Forchia register images."],
  ["Was the cross for gallantry?","Rome, 14 September 1938",
   "The war itself is settled: the family's own papers hold the Croce al Merito di Guerra conferred on «all'operaio Zampiello Angelo di Tommaso» for operations in East Africa from 3 October 1935. What is NOT settled is the family's account that it was for saving many lives. A merit cross is given for service in a theatre; gallantry would ordinarily carry a Medaglia al Valor Militare instead. The bravery may be true and separately awarded, or it may be the family's gloss on this certificate.",
   "His foglio matricolare at the Archivio di Stato di Benevento — which would also give the First World War service of a man born in 1895, entirely untraced."],
  ["Angelo Zampiello's birth record","31 August 1895, Arpaia",
   "His birth date is now corroborated by his memorial card, but the Arpaia births of 1895 are one of the two volumes not digitised, so the act itself is still unread.",
   "Ordering the volume at the Archivio di Stato di Benevento. See the Antenati page."],
  ["When did Carminantonio and Filomena arrive?","Brisbane — a candidate at last",
   "Both died in Brisbane, and for a long time neither appeared in any passenger index. FindMyPast's copy of the National Archives passenger lists now gives one: CARMINE FALCO, arrived BRISBANE on 26 MARCH 1964 aboard the SYDNEY, departure port NAPLES — the port that serves Arienzo. NAA barcode 12100311. A FILOMENA FALCO arrives on 5 February 1968, NAA barcode 9788535, but that entry names no ship, no port and no state, so it does not even place her in Queensland. Separately, the Giuseppe Falco who reached Brisbane per the Palermo in 1925 is NOT the Giuseppe Falco of Queensland in these records — that one was born about 1898, married a Piedmontese wife and lived and died in the Burdekin.",
   "LEADS, NOT FINDINGS: the index gives no age, no birth year and no travelling companion, and Carmine Falco is not a rare name among Campanian emigrants. What settles it is the image — barcode 12100311 at NAA RecordSearch, which carries age, occupation, last address and often the nominator. Ordinary work, not a blocked door."],
  ["Two Carmine Annecchinos","Forchia, 1883",
   "One with a wife Maria Carfora, one with Maria Giovanna Crisci — and children twelve days apart in September 1883. Two men, or an indexing error.",
   "The Forchia baptisms, read directly."],
  ["Which Raffaele is generation four? — ANSWERED","Arienzo, 1816, 1817 and 1818",
   "Vincenzo Falco and Andreana Crisci named a son RAFFAELE twice: act 56 of 25 April 1816, and act 47 of 10 May 1818, both born in their own house on strada Camellara. Ordinarily the first of two children sharing a name has died before the second is named — but no burial had been found, so this archive carried generation four as DISPUTED rather than assert the likelier answer. The 1817 death register settles it. On 28 OCTOBER 1817, «Raffaele Falco figlio di Vincenzo Falco, e d'Andreana Crisci» was declared dead at seven in the morning, aged about eighteen months. That is the child of April 1816. The Raffaele of 10 May 1818 is the one who lived, married Angela Rosa Morgillo and died in 1880. Generation four is documented.",
   "Closed — and it closed something else on the way. The burial was declared by GIUSEPPE FALCO, 37, massaro di campagna of strada Camellara, who with Sabato Vassallo is recorded as a «PARENTE» of the family. Giuseppe is a documented son of Matteo, which would make him this child's great-uncle. It is the second time the Arienzo registers use the word kin of a Falco declarant on that street. What remains is the age problem the two birth acts leave behind: Vincenzo is thirty-five in 1816 and twenty-seven in 1818."],
  ["Arcangelo Crisci, twice — ANSWERED","Arienzo, 1817 and 1820",
   "He appeared both as Andreana Crisci's father and as the husband of Anna Falco: one man twice married, or two men? The 1817 birth register settles it — TWO Arcangelo Crisci declare births in that single year, one aged 52 and one aged 30. And the 1820 death register now says who Anna Falco was. She died on 23 August 1820, aged twenty-five, «moglie di ARCANGELO CRISCI … FIGLIA DI PASQUALE FALCO … E DI CHIARA RIVETTI» — a daughter of this line's own generation two, and a sister of Vincenzo. Seven months earlier she had buried a two-day-old son, Fortunato Crisci, on strada Camellara.",
   "Closed. The younger Arcangelo, born about 1787, married the line's own Anna Falco, born about 1795; the elder, born about 1765, is Andreana's father. Two men, and both of them married a Falco or fathered a Falco's wife. Note that this archive previously said Anna died «at 22» — the act says TWENTY-FIVE."],
  ["The unnamed child","25 November 1777",
   "Matteo and Francesca baptised a child that day whose name the transcription does not carry.",
   "The register image."],
  ["Was Chiara Rivetti gentry?","Arienzo, 1816 and 1843",
   "Chiara Rivetti, who married Pasquale Falco, is called at her death in 1843 «filia quondam Nicolai et Anna» — the only record naming her parents. The 1816 death register has DONNA FRANCESCA RIVETTI, «figlia del fu Don Nicola, e Donn'Anna Quotolo, moglie di Don Claudio Cimmino». The same two forenames, in the same parish, one generation apart. If Francesca and Chiara are sisters, then Chiara's mother was ANNA QUOTOLO and the Rivetti of this line were Don and Donna — which would make Pasquale Falco, a contadino, the son-in-law of a gentry house.",
   "It is a lead and not a finding, and reading the 1816-1818 death registers in full has made it WEAKER rather than stronger. Those three volumes turn up Rivetti at every level of the town: bracciali on strada Camellara, vaticali on strada Sant'Andrea, a Don, and DON GIUSEPPE RIVETTI, a priest of forty-one in 1818. Three generations of one Rivetti household — Giuseppe and Andreana Sossi, their son Paolo, his daughter Maria — are contadini. The surname spans the whole social range of Arienzo, so a shared pair of forenames proves nothing about which Rivetti Chiara belonged to. Add that the 1843 Latin entry gives her parents no honorific, and that a house marrying one daughter to a proprietario is unlikely to marry another to a day-labourer. What would settle it: the Rivetti baptisms, or Chiara's own marriage record, which would name her father with or without the Don."],
  ["Angela Falco's death of 1829 — PROBABLY SOLVED","Arienzo — parish register vs civil register",
   "This archive relied on a parish entry read through machine transcription in which Pasquale Falco is named WITHOUT «quondam» on the death of a daughter ANGELA, dated 1829 — evidence he was then alive. But the 1829 civil death register, read image by image, contains no Angela Falco and no Falco of any name. THE 1820 REGISTER NOW OFFERS A BETTER CANDIDATE: on 23 August 1820 ANNA FALCO, twenty-five, died on strada Camellara, and her act names her father Pasquale Falco without «fu» — exactly the shape of the entry the archive was relying on. Anna against Angela, 1820 against 1829: both are the kind of slip a machine transcription makes.",
   "Very likely the same event, misread and misdated. What would settle it: the parish register image for 1820 and for 1829, read directly rather than through the transcription. Either way the point is now moot for the line — Pasquale is independently documented alive in August 1820 and dead on 1 October 1834."],
  ["The siblings who stayed in Italy","Arienzo, 1898 and 1913",
   "Raffaele Falco and Costanza Maione had children at Arienzo besides Carminantonio, who emigrated — Giovanna in 1898 and Gerardo in 1913. Neither has been traced beyond their birth record. Their descendants would be the closest living relatives of this family still in Campania, and nobody has looked for them.",
   "The Arienzo civil registers after 1900, and the Caserta indexes; ordinary research, not a blocked door."],
  ["The emigrant generation is only half followed","Brisbane, 1925–1972",
   "Carminantonio's son Raffaele reached Brisbane, married Caterina Zampiello and is buried at Nudgee; his family has not been worked through. Four Zampiello sisters reached Queensland and two married out, into Servodio and Miceli — both surnames are named in the immigration files and then dropped.",
   "The NAA files already identified, and Queensland records."],
  ["Two daughters called Maria","1789 and 1790",
   "Pasquale and Chiara named a daughter Maria in September 1789 and again in October 1790. Ordinarily that means the first died. No burial has been found.",
   "The Arienzo death register, 1789–90."],
];
---
<Base title="Open questions">
  <h1>Open questions</h1>
  <p class="lede">What this archive does not know, why it does not know it, and — where it matters — what
  it would take to find out. Published because the person holding the missing piece is more likely to be
  a stranger than the person who wrote this.</p>

  <h2>Doors that need a person</h2>
  <p>These are not research problems. They are things a machine cannot do and a human can.</p>
  {blocked.map(([t,w,d,who]) => (
    <section class="card" style="margin:1rem 0">
      <h3 style="margin-bottom:.2rem">{t}</h3>
      <p class="small mono" style="margin:.1rem 0 .5rem">{w}</p>
      <p style="margin:.2rem 0">{d}</p>
      <p style="margin:.5rem 0 0"><strong>What would open it:</strong> {who}</p>
    </section>
  ))}

  <h2>Questions the records have not answered</h2>
  {questions.map(([t,w,d,how]) => (
    <section class="card" style="margin:1rem 0">
      <h3 style="margin-bottom:.2rem">{t}</h3>
      <p class="small mono" style="margin:.1rem 0 .5rem">{w}</p>
      <p style="margin:.2rem 0">{d}</p>
      <p class="small" style="margin:.5rem 0 0"><strong>Where to look:</strong> {how}</p>
    </section>
  ))}

  <h2>Where the searching has already failed</h2>
  <p>Recorded so that the next attempt does not repeat it. Matteo Falco's marriage was searched across
  2,056 transcribed marriage and death pages, including 173 carrying 1750s Latin date-words and 58
  carrying 1760s ones. Filomena Annecchino's birth was searched across 681 harvested Annecchino records.
  Angelo Zampiello's birth was searched to the limit of FamilySearch's deep-paging cap in both
  directions. <strong>Trove is a dead end for this family</strong> — its newspapers largely stop at 1955,
  before these people arrived in Australia.</p>

  <h2>If you are a Falco</h2>
  <p>There are other Arienzo Falco households in these registers that have not been joined to this
  line — Francesco &amp; Maria Angela Ulivieccio, Giuseppe &amp; Gelsumina Vigliotta, Antonio &amp;
  Carmela Guida, Vincenzo &amp; Angela Pellone, Angelo &amp; Maria Cimmino, Nicola &amp; Carmela
  Terracciano. Several are probably cousins. If one of them is yours, the
  <a href={u("/households")}>households</a> are the place to start, and a correction is the most
  valuable thing you can send.</p>
</Base>
